<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ForegroundTracker: the detector that cannot read a screen.

  `canRetrieveWindowContent="false"`, and that single bit is the reason this service exists. Android
  applies the privilege per *service*: a device whose only enabled accessibility service cannot read
  a window is a device where payment and banking apps have nothing to object to. This is what
  "app-only mode" means, and it is the mode a fresh install is offered.

  `typeWindowStateChanged` alone is all this needs. It carries the foreground package name and
  nothing else, which is exactly the input the app blocker wants.

  Deliberately absent, and each absence is load-bearing:

    - `canRetrieveWindowContent` — see above. With it false there is no `rootInActiveWindow` to call.
    - `flagReportViewIds` — there are no view ids to look up.
    - `typeWindowContentChanged` — no content is read, so content events would be pure wake-ups.
    - `android:isAccessibilityTool` — Curfew does not assist anyone with a disability. Because it is
      not a tool, Android 14 and later hides from it any view an app marks
      `accessibilityDataSensitive` — password fields and payment credentials — which is an
      OS-enforced privacy floor rather than a promise Curfew makes about itself. Setting it true
      would silently remove that floor.

  What this mode gives up, and the user is told before choosing: no URL, site or keyword rules, and
  no uninstall guard — the guard reads a window to recognise Curfew's own removal screen, and this
  service has no window to read. Device admin is the remaining way to resist uninstall, and it is the
  option that costs banking apps instead.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="false"
    android:description="@string/foreground_tracker_description"
    android:notificationTimeout="0" />
